  "_commit": {
    "id": "13f02cb25bb716b1bdad14c28c0c9bc30cb9c53e",
    "author": "marcorotanegroni <marco.rotanegroni@phd.unipd.it>",
    "committer": "marcorotanegroni <marco.rotanegroni@phd.unipd.it>",
    "message": "Warnings for own reads, archived-source links, CITATION (0.99.9)\n\nAligned to Python selexprep v0.4.8 (doi:10.5281/zenodo.24427025). No call,\nstatus or extracted read changes.\n\nWarnings and messages:\n- selexprep_detect(): a called primer that contains a sequencing adapter\n  (warning); a one-sided extraction mode and what the sequences keep\n  (message).\n- extraction: both primers set with a one-sided mode, so one is left\n  untrimmed; a supplied primer that is not in the reads, naming its reverse\n  complement when that one is.\n- run_selexprep(): primers split between R1 and R2, nothing counted.\n- read_selexprep_inputs(): FASTQ files with no reads; a missing path is named.\n\nSupplied primers may carry IUPAC codes, as the help already said. An invalid\nextraction_mode lists the valid values.\n\nHelp pages, vignette and README explain how to run on your own reads: reads\nlonger than the construct, how to write the primers, supplied-primer status,\nreplicates, run time, counting both read orientations.\n\nCatalog provenance links point to the archived release\n(doi:10.5281/zenodo.23104868) and to Software Heritage permalinks of the\nsource commit; the dataset and its metadata are unchanged. Add inst/CITATION.\n",
    "time": 1795293760
  },
